592186563575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563650 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563650 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563650 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)={y}^{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨4,-3⟩?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563650 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fb365d6-3918-560a-af36-ae398310b595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563650 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x{y}^{x-1}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,e) & =⟨{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,1{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇g(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{e(4)+1(-3)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563689 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563689 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563689 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563689 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563689 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56172e38-6320-5f34-8b75-aab1067f37e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563689 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{2}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4) & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction of $∇g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563713 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563713 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563713 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563713 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,-2)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563713 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8889b56a-bb69-56d7-b6a6-42f214a1a3f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563713 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x-yz,\\,2y-xz,\\,2z-xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,0,-2) & =⟨2(1)-0(-2),2(0)-1(-2),2(-2)-1(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2,2,-4⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563741 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563741 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563741 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563741 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5075c32-f624-572f-a2d3-78616466d14e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563741 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16{x}^{3}-8xy,\\,2y-4{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =⟨16(-1)^{3}-8(-1)(1),\\,2(1)-4(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇f(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563750 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563750 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563750 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(-1,3)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i+j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563750 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df6440e4-6b40-5b4e-87c9-62e58eb89d0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563750 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,3) & =(2(-1)-6(3))\\,i+(2(3)-6(-1))j \\\\  & =-20\\,i+12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,3) & =∇\\,f(-1,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-20\\,i+12\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{-20(1)+12(1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563760 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=2i+j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7872fd6-9c54-5e48-b08b-71d8ca0a2063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563760 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+\\frac{y}{x})\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563763 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffacb628-f280-51e0-9811-c0f525cc08ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563763 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+4x-\\frac{1}{x})i-x{sec}^{2}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{1})i-1\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-4\\,i-2\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
